ese have only been available for a  couple of years now  just before he launches the drone stefan  miller gives final instructions to the  riders  on the set he works with a partner  christophe dundas controls the drone  it has a range of 300 meters miller  works the camera himself  he receives the video images live over a  small antenna  concentration is imperative a crash  could be dangerous and expensive  the drone and camera cost a good hundred  thousand euros the rotors are razor  sharpening  the batteries have a duration of six  minutes so they have to be changed often  but for safety's sake the drone only  spends three minutes in the air  stefan and kustoff have to plan their  shoots well to get lots of new  perspectives and angles  on  yes  with a real helicopter you probably  wouldn't be able to fly this low  and if you did you'd stir up lots of  sand  to fly a helicopter a meter off the  ground wouldn't make much sense  this parking garage would also be a  no-go area for a helicopter plus at 4  000 euros an hour a helicopter can be  prohibitively expensive for the same  money you can hire a camera drone for a  day  and the octocopter is regularly booked  out the berlin company's customers  include some major hollywood studios  you'll never be able to shoot a movie  only with octocopters apart from maybe a  bmx film  it's much more of a tool that offers  lots of opportunities  you have to look at where you can use it  and how  for the dirt bikers the drone was  certainly worth it without it  the filmmakers wouldn't have put  together shots like these  in practically no time at all  and that brings us to the end of the  show for today but i'll be back again  tomorrow to kickstart a brand new week  of euromax so until then take care and  goodbye  you
 

